  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through Friday)\nIssued at 303 PM CDT SAT SEP 5 2026\n\nThis afternoon an upper level ridge is stretched from Northern\nMexico into the Central Plains with weak northeast flow over\nCentral Alabama. In the lower levels, weak surface ridging was\npresent over the Western Atlantic and into the Gulf. Convection\nhas been moving through the weak easterly flow since this\nmorning, but is more scattered this afternoon. Temperatures were\nlower this morning with all of the activity, but are warming up\npost convection. One more day of extreme temperatures is\nexpected. A surface boundary is expected to move into the area\nfrom the north/ northeast on Sunday with a medium chance of \nconvection developing along and south of this boundary. There is a\nlevel 1 out of 5 risk for severe storms Sunday afternoon and \nevening across generally the southern half of Central Alabama with\ngusty winds being the main threat. Monday into Tuesday, the upper\nridge expands eastward across more of Conus with the best chances\nfor convection across the southern parts of the CWA closer to the\nsurface boundary and any upper disturbances moving westward \naround the base of the upper ridge. Guidance is suggesting a break\ndown of the upper ridge by mid week as large scale troughing \ndevelops along the Mississippi River Valley out of Canada. Toward \nlate week, northwest flow across the Central US should help to \npush another front toward the Southeast US.\n\nWill forgo extending the heat advisory into Monday at this time.\nThe surface boundary should help to get temperatures down a\ncouple of degrees and a little drier air filtering into the area.\nWith that said, it will still be hot, just maybe right below what\nwe normally see for a heat advisory.\n\n08",
